#Problem APEX 5.2.15

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Applications of integration)
## DBsection(Distance, velocity, acceleration)
## Institution(Valdosta State University)
## KEYWORDS(definite integral, velocity, displacement)
## Author(S. V. Ault)
## Level(5)
## TitleText1('APEX Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hartman')
## EditionText1('3.0')
## Section1('5.2')
## Problem1('15')
## Updated July 2023

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl',
    'plots.pl',      'parserNumberWithUnits.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);
Context('Numeric');

($x1, $y1) = (0, random(3, 8));
($x2, $y2) = (random(2, 4), $y1);
($x3, $y3) = ($x2 + random(2, 4), 0);
($x4, $y4) = ($x3 + random(2, 4), random(3, 8));
($x5, $y5) = ($x4 + random(4, 7), $y4);

$f1 = "$y1";
$m2 = ($y3 - $y2) / ($x3 - $x2);
$f2 = "$m2*(x - $x2) + $y2";

$m3 = ($y4 - $y3) / ($x4 - $x3);
$f3 = "$m3*(x - $x3) + $y3";

$f4 = "$y4";

$plot = Plot(
    xmin            => -1,
    xmax            => 10,
    xtick_delta     => 2,
    xminor          => 1,
    xlabel          => '\(t\)',
    ymin            => -1,
    ymax            => 10,
    ytick_delta     => 2,
    yminor          => 1,
    ylabel          => '\(v(t)\)',
    rounded_corners => 1
);
$plot->add_dataset(
    [ $x1, $y1 ],
    [ $x2, $y2 ],
    [ $x3, $y3 ],
    [ $x4, $y4 ],
    [ $x5, $y5 ],
    color => 'blue'
);

$b2 = $x3 - $x2;
$b3 = $x4 - $x3;
$b4 = $x5 - $x4;

$ans1 = NumberWithUnits($y1 > $y5 ? $y1 : $y5, 'm/s');
$ans2 = NumberWithUnits(
    $x2 * $y1 + (1 / 2) * $b2 * $y2 + (1 / 2) * $b3 * $y4 + $b4 * $y4, 'm');

$alttext =
    qq/A graph of v(t), which is a continuous set of line segments connecting
the points ($x1,$y1), ($x2,$y2), ($x3,$y3), ($x4,$y4) and ($x5, $y5)./;

BEGIN_PGML
    >> [! [$alttext] !]{$plot}{400} <<

A graph of the velocity function [`v(t)`] of an object moving in
a straight line is given above.  Velocity is measured in [`\textrm{m/s}`], and
time in seconds. 

Include units in all answers. [@ helpLink('units') @]*

a) What is the object's maximum velocity? 
[_]{$ans1}{20}

b) What is the object's maximum displacement? 
[_]{$ans2}{20}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) The maximum velocity is the highest value on the graph,
[` v = [$ans1] `] m/s.

b) Since [` v \geq 0`] on [` [[$xmin], [$xmax]] `], the maximum displacement
is the entire area under the curve on this interval.  Break up
the integral into rectangles and triangles.
    [```
  \begin{aligned}
  \int_{[$xmin]}^{[$xmax]} v(t) \, dt
  &= 
  \int_{[$x1]}^{[$x2]} v(t) \, dt +
  \int_{[$x2]}^{[$x3]} v(t) \, dt + 
  \int_{[$x3]}^{[$x4]} v(t) \, dt + 
  \int_{[$x4]}^{[$x5]} v(t) \, dt \\
  &= ([$x2])([$y1]) + \frac{1}{2}([$b2])([$y2])
  + \frac{1}{2}([$b3])([$y4]) + ([$b4])([$y4]) \\
  &= [$ans2]
  \end{aligned}
    ```]

    The units are m (meters) because displacement is a distance measurement.
END_PGML_SOLUTION
ENDDOCUMENT();

